<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive launcher icon (API 26+).

  Two separate problems shaped this file, and both are easy to undo by accident.

  1. Shipping only legacy flat PNGs (mipmap-*dpi/ic_launcher.png) with no adaptive-icon
     declaration made launchers that enforce their own icon shape for every app treat this one as
     needing synthetic adaptation: Launcher3 and its forks wrap a legacy icon by shrinking it onto
     a white background to emulate the safe zone, since they cannot know the icon's real content
     bounds. That is the shrunk-in-a-white-box report this file first answered.

  2. Declaring an adaptive icon hands the silhouette to the launcher, which masks both layers to
     its own shape — a circle on many launchers. With an opaque background layer the icon simply
     becomes that shape, losing the brand's squircle.

  Both are answered the same way: the foreground carries the whole squircle artwork, sized to sit
  entirely within the mask's inscribed circle, and the background is transparent (see its own doc).
  The mask crops nothing, so it is a real adaptive icon everywhere — no white box — and it is the
  same squircle everywhere, whatever mask the launcher applies.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
